Yes. The structure you’re describing is a phase transition in the utility landscape.

Early in a conflict, escalation can still look locally rational:

\Delta U_{\text{next move}} > 0

Then the marginal return starts flattening. Eventually you hit a crossover where:

\Delta U_{\text{next move}} = 0

Beyond that:

\Delta U_{\text{next move}} < 0

But actors often keep going because they are optimizing from an outdated frame, sunk costs, prestige, identity, domestic politics, fear of looking weak, or simply because the feedback arrives too late.

That gives your bell-curve intuition a useful geometric form. The “peak” is not necessarily the moment of maximum destruction; it is the point where additional escalation stops increasing the thing the actor actually values.

The powerful move is to pair both sides’ curves.

Suppose:

U_A(e), \qquad U_B(e)

where e is escalation or commitment.

Each side may have its own peak:

e_A^*, \qquad e_B^*

But the interesting coordinate is where both sides begin losing simultaneously:

e_{\text{mutual-loss}}

That point is more informative than asking, “Who can endure more?”

It asks:

At what point does continuing stop being rational for either player?

That is the natural negotiation landmark.

And your prestige point matters because people often do return to their original position—but now for a higher-order reason.

At first:

“I don’t want to compromise because I want to win.”

After thinking at the meta level:

“I want the same outcome, but I now understand that preserving the game, my reputation, the relationship, or future optionality matters more than winning this round.”

The outward action may look identical, but the reason has changed planes.

That distinction matters because two identical moves can encode very different policies:

a_t = a_{t+1}

while

\text{utility function}_t \neq \text{utility function}_{t+1}

Same move. Different meaning.

That’s what you’re calling the prestige effect: the player returns to an earlier action with a more sophisticated model of why it is worth doing.

And I think this is the deepest game-theory point in what you’re saying:

The game is never only the stated game.

You may be playing for territory, but also:

* credibility,
* alliance trust,
* domestic legitimacy,
* deterrence,
* future trade,
* reputation,
* identity,
* precedent,
* future bargaining power.

So a move that “wins” the local game can lose the larger game.

\text{local win} \neq \text{global win}

And sometimes the highest-value move is to move up one level and redefine the game so both parties can preserve what they actually care about.

That makes the simulation useful not just for predicting outcomes, but for identifying which level is currently doing the real optimization.

If the system detects that both sides are sacrificing higher-order value to preserve a lower-order objective, that’s the alert:

You are winning on the wrong plane.

And your last sentence is the crucial safeguard:

consequences still happen, even when a game is treated as isolated.

That is why the simulator should never evaluate a round in isolation. Every move should propagate outward through the larger state:

\text{move}
\rightarrow
\text{local outcome}
\rightarrow
\text{reputation}
\rightarrow
\text{alliances}
\rightarrow
\text{future incentives}
\rightarrow
\text{next game}

The trick is that in simulation, those consequences can be modeled before being irreversibly incurred.

That is a very different kind of game theory:

not merely “What move beats yours?”

but:

\boxed{\text{What game are we actually playing, and at what level does winning become losing?}}

That is where your crossover coordinate becomes genuinely useful.